entifier":"forc_explainer","label":"forc_explainer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"soJHUWjuYr"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"iL1E8jAxD6"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"forc-explainer","key":"xoq58IuVbp"},{"type":"text","value":"d).","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"P7N8T9pzOr"}],"key":"LoRtcLCsPt"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/RockmagPy-notebooks/build/b7ae46d3577291fbea29bbc94f5acac4.png","alt":"What a FORC measurement is and how the distribution follows from it, shown with the Baraboo hematite vein material example distributed with RockmagPy. Moments are in absolute units throughout: this specimen does not reach saturation within the measured field range, so there is no meaningful Ms to normalize by. (a) One reversal curve (blue) within the major hysteresis loop (grey). The curve departs the descending branch at the reversal field Ha (open circle) and is measured with increasing applied field Hb; each measurement is one value M(Ha, Hb) of the surface (red), where Ha identifies the curve as a whole and Hb the position along it. Neither branch is a schematic: the ascending branch is the lowest reversal curve and the descending branch is the locus of the points at which the 269 curves begin. Only the segment above the highest reversal field is unmeasured, and is supplied by the inversion symmetry of the loop, with which the measured branches agree to 0.08% of the peak moment where they meet. (b) The full family of curves. (c) The individual measurements plotted at their own (Hb, Ha) and coloured by moment. Each reversal curve is one horizontal row of points, and data exist only where Hb ≥ Ha, so they fill a half-plane bounded by the diagonal. The rows stop at Ha = +95 mT because that is the highest reversal field this acquisition used: reversing from a field above the switching range simply retraces the descending branch, giving a curve with no hysteresis and hence no contribution to ρ. The left-to-right gradient is the high-field slope, which carries no information about ρ. (d) The FORC distribution ρ = −½ ∂²M/∂Ha∂Hb on those same axes, estimated by local quadratic regression. The rotated coordinates in which FORC diagrams are conventionally drawn are overlaid as arrows. Bu runs parallel to the diagonal and Bc at right angles to it, so the diagonal Hb = Ha is simultaneously the edge of the measured region and the Bc = 0 axis. Generated by FORC_notebooks/scripts/make_forc_explainer_figure.py.","width":"100%","align":"center","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"tFt3ZVAg0Q","urlSource":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PmagPy/RockmagPy-notebooks/main/book/images/forc_explainer.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"forc_explainer","identifier":"forc_explainer","html_id":"forc-explainer","enumerator":"1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"MJSfgD506q"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"JhEQrtYKEP"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"Xk52WhXv50"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"SDnnZJeXpU"},{"type":"text","value":"What a FORC measurement is and how the distribution follows from it, shown with the Baraboo hematite vein material example distributed with RockmagPy. Moments are in absolute units throughout: this specimen does not reach saturation within the measured field range, so there is no meaningful M","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"snBfeugAHz"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"s","key":"eubJVTaq5g"}],"key":"sQ0Jqcs02r"},{"type":"text","value":" to normalize by. (a) One reversal curve (blue) within the major hysteresis loop (grey). The curve departs the descending branch at the reversal field H","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1}